# Teaching Molecular Biological Techniques in a Research Content

**Authors:** Stiller, John W., Coggins, T. Chad

## Abstract
Molecular biological methods, such as the polymerase chain reaction (PCR) and gel electrophoresis, are now commonly taught to students in introductory biology courses at the college and even high school levels. This often includes hands-on experience with one or more molecular techniques as part of a general biology laboratory. To assure that most students achieve success, molecular methods are generally introduced in well-rehearsed labs that have undergone thorough trouble-shooting. Unfortunately these sorts of "canned labs" tend to be somewhat divorced from the framework of a real-life biological investigation; that is, students usually learn protocols that are removed from the kind of scientific problem solving for which the applications were designed. In this article, the authors present the design of a research-based laboratory course that employs a process called "environmental PCR" to investigate planktonic community composition and determine whether different results are obtained from visual versus molecular examinations. This lab is embedded within an upper division university-level course, and the total experiment undoubtedly is beyond the resources and time constraints of most high school and introductory biology courses. Nevertheless, the authors offer their experience as an example of just how easily many techniques, previously considered to be too complex for high school and introductory college-level students, can be accomplished in a teaching laboratory. (Contains 6 figures.)

